WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.650
The Council of the
Arts is and will

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:14.330
remain, in my opinion, one of
the most important legacies

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.730
of Dr. Jerome Wiesner.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.320
Council for the Arts at MIT
was founded by Kay Stratton

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.300
and Jerry Wiesner.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.530
Jerry was totally
devoted to the arts,

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:32.340
so that he picked up
the little art committee

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.720
and made it into the
Council for the Arts.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.020
Jerry was a very strong,
anti-nuclear person.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.100
He was President
Kennedy's science advisor,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.010
and felt that
institutions like MIT

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:49.940
were turning out a lot
of students and graduates

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.670
of very high technical
expertise who

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.350
had a fairly large probability
of destroying the world.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.760
And it was very important to
get involved in things that

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.670
have some sense of humanity.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.914
They look good.

00:01:06.914 --> 00:01:07.910
This is nice.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:08.904
That's real nice.

00:01:08.904 --> 00:01:09.404
Yeah.

00:01:09.404 --> 00:01:10.400
That's a beauty.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.880
They found that MIT really
had tremendous strength

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.950
in the arts, and that
the arts perhaps were not

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.130
getting the kind of resonance,
the kind of visibility

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.710
they should and ought to
have, because they thought

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.710
of the arts as very much
part of the mainstream of MIT

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.470
very much part of
the core mission.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.410
When the council was
created 40 years ago,

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.510
it was almost unique.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.120
There may have been one
or two others of its kind.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.740
Where else but at MIT?

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.355
We like going first.

00:01:40.355 --> 00:01:43.253
[CLASSICAL PIANO MUSIC]

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:49.532
[TECHNO MUSIC]

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.970
The view at that point, which
is very strange looking back,

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.220
is if we're going to
do art, let's buy some

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.980
and put it out in the campus.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.280
In retrospect,
that was brilliant.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.560
Public art collections
on university campuses

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:10.630
were ranked back in 2006,
and MIT was in the top 10,

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:15.070
and we love showing off
this public art collection.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.890
The two most recent
pieces acquired

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.900
was Anish Kapoor's
sculpture, which

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.750
is over in the Stata
Center and Cai Guo-Qiang's

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:23.650
at the Sloan School.

00:02:28.996 --> 00:02:32.398
[UPBEAT DRUMMING]

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.342
The grants program was the
most vital thing CAMIT does.

00:02:43.342 --> 00:02:44.800
Over the course of
the years, we've

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.420
given over 1,000 grants away,
several million dollars.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.630
A lot of the money we
give allows these people

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.810
to get money from other people
probably two to three to one.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.460
These grants impacted
my work in ways

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.860
that I had to think
about community.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:09.970
A mask that maybe--

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.587
These grants are meant to
provide for interaction

00:03:12.587 --> 00:03:13.795
between students and faculty.

00:03:13.795 --> 00:03:16.390
We think it's almost exposed
and it's just an [INAUDIBLE]----

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.835
This is where a lot of
my work has evolved.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.950
The Council is very important
that it supports the arts

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:31.330
here in many different aspects
in carrying the word of art

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.780
to the world and allowing
us to develop and flourish

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.430
in the Institute.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:37.900
So thank you.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.890
CAMIT's support of the MIT
Museum and the List Visual Arts

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:49.090
Center is another
wonderful dimension.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.590
We give money to the BSO so
that MIT students could get

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:55.930
tickets-- they're
either free or cheap--

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.345
money to the Gardner
Museum, and to the MFA.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.205
We give money every year to the
Artist-in-Residence program.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.290
All of my interactions
with the council

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.660
have been student-focused.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.270
We've had residencies where
artists will come in and work

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.140
with the ensembles.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.330
Joe Lovano is probably one of
the greatest tenor saxophone

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:37.530
soloists in jazz, and we were
able to get him to come to MIT.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:44.710
And the students
rose to the occasion.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.570
It was a wonderful
example of how,

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.880
when they're in the
presence of a great master,

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.890
they just lift
and lift and lift.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.000
The McDermott Award was
founded by Margaret McDermott,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.090
the widow of Eugene
McDermott, the founder

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.860
of Texas Instruments.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.130
Mrs. McDermott decided,
after Eugene died,

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:11.760
that she would like to give
an award every year to artists

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.650
that are mid-career.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.840
Let me confess.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.390
I love Santa Domingo.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.150
I love coming home to
the guys in blazers

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.790
trying to push little cups
of Brugal into my hands.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.460
And it has attracted
wonderful people.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.830
Junot Diaz, who
we later recruited

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.380
to our faculty, a prominent
New Yorker writer,

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.120
Pulitzer Prize winner.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.160
Diller and Scofidio,
two architects

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:39.870
who were unknown at the time
they were brought to campus,

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:42.240
later became world
class architects,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.200
and are among other things
the architects of the ICA.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.930
In all cases, the
people who get the award

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:54.300
spend some time at MIT with
the students in some classes.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.160
It's like learning
baseball from Babe Ruth.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:01.300
But don't be nervous.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:02.750
Da-da da-da da.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.250
Da-da da-da da-da da-da-da na.

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.250
Da-da da-da dum.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.201
Da-da da-da dum.

00:06:07.201 --> 00:06:08.088
Da-da da-da dum.

00:06:08.088 --> 00:06:09.005
[LAUGHS MISCHIEVOUSLY]

00:06:09.005 --> 00:06:09.907
[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]

00:06:09.907 --> 00:06:10.809
You know?

00:06:10.809 --> 00:06:11.735
Four and--

00:06:11.735 --> 00:06:13.160
[ORCHESTRA PLAYS]

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:15.680
The young conductor of the
Los Angeles Philharmonic,

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.190
Gustavo Dudamel, came
here to receive the award.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.030
When an audience--

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:23.660
Most recently, Robert Lepage,
the Canadian theater maestro,

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:25.130
everything, he does at all.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:28.130
With experience we've
learned that we should not

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.390
force connections.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.430
We let them, and eventually
two or three things

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.070
seems to go together, and we
go, oh, there's a pattern there.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.800
And so the award has really
evolved from a nice award,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.310
to an important,
intermediate Artist Award,

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.770
to one of the highest
level awards in the world.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.810
CAMIT has always
been an organization

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.720
that socially bonds,
that goes on trips,

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.820
has wonderful art
trips getting into very

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.980
exclusive private galleries.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.080
We've been to Prague,
we've been to Berlin,

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.280
we've been to Chicago.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.330
[MUSIC]

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:04.830
It's not at the
cutting edge of art,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.600
it's at the cutting
edge of people.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.660
I mean you're meeting
very smart people.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.700
Among the most
important reasons one

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.360
should join CAMIT
is you get closer

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.080
to a truly great institution
of higher learning.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.030
[GLASS CLINKING]

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:25.680
At MIT, we are very
much oriented toward art

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.770
at the intersection of
science and technology.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:34.440
To find out what can we do
that will change the world.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.930
The best works that are
developed in our classes

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.870
here come from students
who are brilliant people

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.240
from mathematics,
material science,

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.850
or mechanical engineering.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:48.390
Art opens their view
and possibilities

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:51.000
beyond the
problem-solving agenda.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.960
[ORCHESTRA PLAYING]

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.970
CAMIT has been generous to MIT.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:00.810
It is the single most
important sponsor of the arts.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.280
[ORCHESTRA PLAYING]

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:07.750
The Council for the Arts at
MIT gives an additional voice

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:10.100
to the arts.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.260
It's this kind of
nourishment that the council

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.440
provides that really makes
a difference in what we're

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:17.450
able to do.

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:21.500
It's about a concern about
what the arts mean at MIT.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.300
[ORCHESTRA PLAYING]

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:47.050
[APPLAUSE]

